#!/usr/bin/python3
"""untrash-mesh - UntrashMesh: UntrashOS devices in one encrypted network.

  untrash-mesh status                 JSON: this node, mesh, members, address book
  untrash-mesh create NAME            start a mesh with this node as its admin
  untrash-mesh invite [HOURS]         one-time invitation (text for the other device)
  untrash-mesh join INVITATION        join the mesh of an invitation
  untrash-mesh remove NODE            take a member out (admins)
  untrash-mesh leave                  leave the mesh (keys stay, peers are dropped)
  untrash-mesh rename NAME            name of this node in the mesh
  untrash-mesh share NODE JSON        co-op: offer shares to member NODE,
                                      {"Photos": {"mode": "ro"|"rw", "path": "/i-data/..."}}
                                      ({} = offer nothing any more)
  untrash-mesh mirror-start VOLPATH LABEL NODE
                                      off-site mirror: ask member NODE to keep a
                                      copy of the volume at VOLPATH (/i-data/<id>)
  untrash-mesh mirror-accept ID VOLPATH  keep the copy in a file on VOLPATH
  untrash-mesh mirror-decline ID | mirror-stop ID
  untrash-mesh mirror-up DEV VOLPATH  at mount time (untrash-import-zyxel): the
                                      mirrored device to mount, or {"error"}
  untrash-mesh invite HOURS USER      an invitation for the UntrashApp, linked to Desk USER
  untrash-mesh app-link NODE USER     link an app member to a Desk user ("" = unlink)
  untrash-mesh run                    the daemon (untrash-mesh.service)

Co-op mode: a node offers some of its shares to a member, read-only or
read/write.  It makes a Samba account for that member (mesh-<id>) and
Samba sections reachable only from the mesh prefix (/etc/samba/
untrash-mesh.conf, included by smb.conf), and sends the offer with the
account over the mesh.  The member mounts each offered share (CIFS, SMB3,
inside WireGuard) at /p-data/<partner>-<share>: a partner volume, on which
its admin can make shares for its own users like on any volume.

Off-site mirror (extended RAID, RAID 1 over the mesh): DRBD, protocol A
(asynchronous) with pull-ahead, so local writes never wait for the line;
DRBD records what changed while the partner was away and sends only that.
The volume stays byte for byte in its format (stock md/LVM/ext4): DRBD's
metadata lives in a file on the state (/state/mirror/<id>.meta, a loop
device).  The partner keeps the copy in a sparse file on a volume it chose
(<volume>/.untrash-mirror/<id>.img) and is never primary.  The mirror starts
with the next mount of the volume (untrash-import-zyxel calls mirror-up); if
DRBD cannot start, the volume is mounted directly and the copy is marked for
a full resync, so it never silently differs.

Links: WireGuard, interface ut-mesh, UDP 51820.  Every node has an IPv6
address in the mesh's ULA prefix (fd00::/8 + 40 bits of the mesh id), the
host part from its signing key; <name>.mesh resolves on every member
(/etc/hosts block).

Trust: a node is its Ed25519 key (slot/zt-sig.c, the same signatures as the
slot images).  The roster (who is in the mesh, with which WireGuard key) is
signed by an admin; the highest roster seq signed by an admin of the current
roster wins.

Address book (no static IP, no DNS needed): every node signs its own
record {endpoints, seq, time}.  When an endpoint changes it raises seq and
pushes the record to every member; every node keeps the newest valid record
per member and forwards records it had not seen, so a change also reaches
nodes the changed node could not reach itself.  A node learns its public
endpoint from its peers: each sync answer says from where the peer saw the
request arrive (WireGuard's endpoint of that peer).  WireGuard roaming does
the rest: a packet from a new address updates the endpoint at once.

Sync: HTTP JSON on the mesh address, port 7340, only from mesh addresses of
members (WireGuard guarantees the source address of a peer).  Join: HTTP on
port 7341, open only while an invitation is pending, every message bound to
the invitation's one-time secret (HMAC-SHA256).

State: /etc/untrash/mesh/ (node.sec/.pub, wg.key, mesh.json, book.json).
Test hooks: ZT_MESH_DIR, ZT_MESH_FAKE_WG (no ip/wg; peers in a JSON file),
ZT_MESH_ADDRMAP (JSON mesh address -> host:port for sync), ZT_MESH_JOIN_HOST,
ZT_MESH_SYNC_PORT, ZT_MESH_JOIN_PORT, ZT_MESH_ENDPOINTS (own endpoints),
ZT_SIG.
"""
